WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.292 --> 00:00:04.500
-Hi, everyone.
I'm Sarah Jenks-Daly,

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.708
supervising producer
at "Late Night"

00:00:06.708 --> 00:00:08.625
and this is "Late Night Lit."

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:14.208
♪♪

00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:22.000
You may know Adrienne Brodeur
from her past work,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.917
in particular,
her best-selling memoir,

00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:27.583
"Wild Game," which was published
a few years ago.

00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:30.750
Her latest is a novel
called "Little Monsters,"

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.208
which is set on Cape Cod

00:00:32.208 --> 00:00:33.875
and is a spectacular
reading choice

00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:36.542
for wherever you find
yourself this summer.

00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:39.792
We're joined by Adrienne to talk
about what surprised her

00:00:39.792 --> 00:00:42.667
when writing a story
from multiple perspectives,

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:44.750
her work as
the executive director

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.667
of the literary
nonprofit Aspen Words.

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:50.250
And if her two teenagers
ever weigh in

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.333
with what they think
of her books.

00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:56.500
Now, here's my chat
with Adrienne Brodeur.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.583
Today, we are joined
by the author

00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:00.500
of the new novel
"Little Monsters."

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.000
Adrian, thanks for being here
on "Late Night Lit."

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.708
-Thanks for having me, Sarah.

00:01:04.708 --> 00:01:07.917
-This novel is terrific.
I really enjoyed it.

00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:10.583
It's a story about a family
living on Cape Cod,

00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:13.000
which is actually where
you are now. Is that right?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.750
-Actually, I'm in Cambridge
right now,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.875
but I do live on Cambridge
and Cape Cod.

00:01:16.875 --> 00:01:18.167
I split my time.

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.375
-Okay.
So the tourist season

00:01:20.375 --> 00:01:22.667
is upon you
or approaching out there.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.083
I wondered, have you thought
about how surreal

00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:26.667
this might be
to walk down the beach

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.708
and see someone reading
your book this summer?

00:01:28.708 --> 00:01:30.750
-I would love
to walk down a beach

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.417
and see someone reading
my book ever.

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:35.292
I've actually never impromptu

00:01:35.292 --> 00:01:38.583
stumbled upon someone
reading a work of mine.

00:01:38.583 --> 00:01:39.750
-I think that's more common

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.292
maybe if you live
in New York City

00:01:41.292 --> 00:01:43.000
and you ride
the subway frequently,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.458
or a place where you're running
into a lot of commuters.

00:01:45.458 --> 00:01:48.875
-There you go.
I'll work on moving.

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:52.625
-I read that you were at least
partially inspired for the story

00:01:52.625 --> 00:01:54.917
by a biblical tale when it came
to the idea

00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:56.083
for "Little Monsters."

00:01:56.083 --> 00:01:57.708
Can you tell us about that?

00:01:57.708 --> 00:02:00.833
-Sure. Well, you know,
it's an interesting process

00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:03.417
how any authors find
their way to their books.

00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:07.000
But I noticed
what I was noticing,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.750
which is basically my mind
or my subconscious

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.292
kept alighting on siblings
and sibling rivalry

00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:18.125
and what made this
sort of relationship

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:22.458
so fraught often
and so ripe for --

00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:24.292
for delving into and exploring.

00:02:24.292 --> 00:02:28.250
And in any case, I thought then
I should go to the original

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.458
sibling-rivalry tale
of Cain and Abel.

00:02:31.458 --> 00:02:34.458
And of course, I got there
and that is the slimmest,

00:02:34.458 --> 00:02:36.417
most bare-bones story on earth.

00:02:36.417 --> 00:02:38.083
I pretty much
got nothing from it.

00:02:38.083 --> 00:02:42.125
I was waiting for, you know,
well, why did, why did.

00:02:42.125 --> 00:02:43.458
But actually, in the end,

00:02:43.458 --> 00:02:45.583
what I took away
from Cain and Abel

00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:47.333
without even
really realizing it,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:51.333
which I'm so grateful for,
was the structure of the book,

00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:53.958
the idea that gifts
would be given

00:02:53.958 --> 00:02:58.292
and this would be a way
to reveal a favoritism

00:02:58.292 --> 00:03:00.667
or a perceived favoritism.

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:03.167
So the momentum
of "Little Monsters"

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:04.875
all works towards this event

00:03:04.875 --> 00:03:08.500
at the summer when
the patriarch is turned 70.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.875
It's his birthday celebration.

00:03:10.875 --> 00:03:12.958
-There are varying
perspectives in the book

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:14.375
you write from.

00:03:14.375 --> 00:03:16.125
I think --
Is it five different char--?

00:03:16.125 --> 00:03:18.500
-Mm-hmm.
-Okay, so Abby and Ken,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.625
the siblings you spoke about,
their father, Adam,

00:03:21.625 --> 00:03:23.833
Steph, who readers will learn
who she is,

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:26.792
and Jenny, who is Ken's wife.

00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:32.542
Was there one perspective
that you were either drawn to

00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:34.750
that was easy for you
to write or alternately

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.583
really difficult for you
to find the headspace for?

00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:40.833
-I love this question
because I feel like

00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:43.500
I've got the weirdest answer,
which is that Adam,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.708
the 70-year-old bipolar
patriarch, just came to me.

00:03:48.708 --> 00:03:52.000
I would literally put my hands
over the keyboard

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.667
and he just he arrived
on the page, fully formed,

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:58.958
sort of this cantankerous,
funny, brilliant,

00:03:58.958 --> 00:04:02.292
you know, dysfunctional,
wonderful guy.

00:04:02.292 --> 00:04:03.583
And he just spoke to me.

00:04:03.583 --> 00:04:05.625
With all the rest
of the characters,

00:04:05.625 --> 00:04:08.583
I mean, they were of varying
difficulty at different moments,

00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:10.292
but it was much more
the traditional you have

00:04:10.292 --> 00:04:12.583
to write sort of halfway
into your novel

00:04:12.583 --> 00:04:14.167
to sort of get
to know them well enough

00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:17.333
to really have them
kind of come easily to you.

00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:20.750
But it was so fun to be

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:23.667
in a very close
third-person point of view

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:26.792
for each of them to be really
deep in their heads

00:04:26.792 --> 00:04:28.583
simply because it was kind of

00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:31.083
this kaleidoscopic look
at this family.

00:04:31.083 --> 00:04:33.292
-Well, that's interesting
because I was struck

00:04:33.292 --> 00:04:35.833
by how each perspective

00:04:35.833 --> 00:04:38.292
was specific,
incredibly specific,

00:04:38.292 --> 00:04:40.708
and also felt consistent
to the character

00:04:40.708 --> 00:04:43.917
and the voice as I was reading
and when I finished.

00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:47.792
So but I guess that might
have taken some time for you

00:04:47.792 --> 00:04:49.667
if that's something
you realize halfway through.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.167
But how did you try to ensure

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:54.667
that each voice did sound
unique enough to you?

00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:56.875
-Really, it is like
getting to know them.

00:04:56.875 --> 00:04:58.625
I mean, I think
there's this thought

00:04:58.625 --> 00:05:01.625
that you approach
a novel kind of with this --

00:05:01.625 --> 00:05:03.250
I mean, you do have
a formed idea,

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.625
but it's this --
it's this combination

00:05:05.625 --> 00:05:08.833
of having to hold tight
to what you know

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:12.583
and not so -- not so tightly
that a lot of stuff,

00:05:12.583 --> 00:05:16.333
a lot of unexpected things
can't happen.

00:05:16.333 --> 00:05:19.000
So it is just what you said.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.625
It is the process
of going for some time,

00:05:22.625 --> 00:05:25.000
getting to know
these characters very well.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.083
But the fun about
being so deeply

00:05:27.083 --> 00:05:30.875
inside the various points
of view is that

00:05:30.875 --> 00:05:34.375
your reader knows more than
any of the other characters do.

00:05:34.375 --> 00:05:38.958
So you've sort of got this very
rich environment to play with.

00:05:38.958 --> 00:05:40.625
-Your previous book

00:05:40.625 --> 00:05:43.167
was an award-winning memoir
called "Wild Game."

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:45.250
I haven't read that yet.
I just want to start off.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.583
I definitely will
now that I've read about it.

00:05:48.583 --> 00:05:50.708
I'd guess that that was
a completely different

00:05:50.708 --> 00:05:53.208
writing experience
than a fictional story.

00:05:53.208 --> 00:05:54.667
But there are some similarities

00:05:54.667 --> 00:05:56.417
from what I read
in terms of the setting

00:05:56.417 --> 00:05:59.000
and maybe honesty
in relationships

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.417
and the secrets that people
keep that you seem like

00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:04.500
you want to continue to explore.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.292
And I was wondering
if that is true.

00:06:06.292 --> 00:06:08.333
Is that something you find you
want to keep writing about?

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.792
-Absolutely.
I think -- I mean,

00:06:10.792 --> 00:06:12.792
as anyone
who has read "Wild Game,"

00:06:12.792 --> 00:06:15.167
I mean, it just will come
as no surprise

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:18.375
that I'm fascinated
by family secrets and that,

00:06:18.375 --> 00:06:23.125
you know, they inhabit
this new book in a big way.

00:06:23.125 --> 00:06:26.208
And I just think
secret keeping -- it's so --

00:06:26.208 --> 00:06:29.250
I mean, "A,"
it's something that most humans

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.000
do on some level or another.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.500
And it just makes for really
compelling fiction or nonfiction

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.375
for that matter, because there's
a tension between the truth,

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:40.917
between what's making you
keep these secrets.

00:06:40.917 --> 00:06:42.792
Is it shame?
Is it being kind?

00:06:42.792 --> 00:06:44.417
Why are you holding
this information?

00:06:44.417 --> 00:06:48.208
And of course, the, "Uh-oh, when
is it gonna come out?"

00:06:48.208 --> 00:06:52.167
Which we all know,
you know, secrets --

00:06:52.167 --> 00:06:55.042
secrets don't like
to stay secret.

00:06:55.042 --> 00:06:57.000
-And sometimes they don't
come out

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.167
at the best times either.
-Absolutely.

00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:00.833
-I was wondering what
the writing process

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:02.667
in general looks like for you.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:07.208
Do you try to set specific time
aside in your day to do it?

00:07:07.208 --> 00:07:11.042
-Yes, I am one
of those discipline writers.

00:07:11.042 --> 00:07:14.917
I do not wait
for the muse to strike.

00:07:14.917 --> 00:07:18.250
I sit down and hope
she can keep up.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:21.875
But yeah, I, you know,
I have two children.

00:07:21.875 --> 00:07:26.000
I've got an elderly father.
I have a job that I love.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.083
I've got a very busy life
like most of us.

00:07:29.083 --> 00:07:33.167
And so I carve time
when no one can bother me,

00:07:33.167 --> 00:07:36.333
which I have found
the perfect time is 5:00 a.m.

00:07:36.333 --> 00:07:40.083
No one -- It's before my own
to-do list can kick in.

00:07:40.083 --> 00:07:44.292
So I just, I get my coffee,
I sit at my desk,

00:07:44.292 --> 00:07:46.292
I either write longhand
or on my computer.

00:07:46.292 --> 00:07:50.625
But it's really just sort
of the witching hour.

00:07:50.625 --> 00:07:52.125
It's the perfect hour for me.

00:07:52.125 --> 00:07:56.125
It's -- it's quiet.
It's that liminal state.

00:07:56.125 --> 00:07:58.917
I think I'm still connected
to my subconscious a bit,

00:07:58.917 --> 00:08:00.500
but it's -- it's perfect.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.375
And that's when
I write every day.

00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:05.958
-Do you find you have to do it
in the same place?

00:08:05.958 --> 00:08:10.792
-No. I mean, I really can write
almost anywhere provided,

00:08:10.792 --> 00:08:12.292
but I'm not good --

00:08:12.292 --> 00:08:14.375
I admire the people
who can go to Starbucks

00:08:14.375 --> 00:08:16.750
and have music
in the background and so on.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:20.625
I have my little weird,
quirky writer rituals

00:08:20.625 --> 00:08:22.625
and have to kind of
just feel right.

00:08:22.625 --> 00:08:26.125
But then I can
I can do it anywhere.

00:08:26.125 --> 00:08:27.875
-You mentioned
your two children.

00:08:27.875 --> 00:08:31.792
Do they read anything you write
or are they interested?

00:08:31.792 --> 00:08:36.083
-Well, you know, my daughter
has read this novel,

00:08:36.083 --> 00:08:39.000
which was really fun because
she hadn't read the memoir,

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.417
which was fine.

00:08:40.417 --> 00:08:42.292
In fact,
I sort of felt proud of the fact

00:08:42.292 --> 00:08:45.000
that she didn't read it,
because I had been,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.000
as you'll know,
if you read the memoir,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.667
I had been so obsessed
and overly involved

00:08:49.667 --> 00:08:51.292
with my own mother
that I thought,

00:08:51.292 --> 00:08:53.500
"Wow, this is great.
She doesn't want to know

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.833
every nuance of my personal
life, and that's fabulous."

00:08:56.833 --> 00:08:59.542
And she loves
really worldbuilding fiction

00:08:59.542 --> 00:09:02.208
and those types of things,
but she read it on spring break

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:05.167
and really loved it,
which was fun.

00:09:05.167 --> 00:09:09.167
My son is 14 and I'd say,
"No, he's not so interested."

00:09:09.167 --> 00:09:13.542
My father was a writer
and I didn't read his work

00:09:13.542 --> 00:09:15.458
until I was in my early 20s.

00:09:15.458 --> 00:09:18.292
I mean, I dipped in.
But, you know, snooze, right?

00:09:18.292 --> 00:09:19.875
You're not interested
in your parents.

00:09:19.875 --> 00:09:21.250
You're interested
in your social life.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:22.667
-Yeah.
I don't think you're interested

00:09:22.667 --> 00:09:24.292
in your parents
until you get older.

00:09:24.292 --> 00:09:25.875
-Yeah.

00:09:25.875 --> 00:09:27.208
-But that's probably
for the best, like you said.

00:09:27.208 --> 00:09:28.458
-Definitely.

00:09:28.458 --> 00:09:30.042
-In addition
to published author,

00:09:30.042 --> 00:09:31.625
you're also -- you mentioned
a full-time job.

00:09:31.625 --> 00:09:32.917
You're the executive director

00:09:32.917 --> 00:09:35.583
of the literary nonprofit
Aspen Words.

00:09:35.583 --> 00:09:36.833
I was hoping you
could talk a little bit

00:09:36.833 --> 00:09:38.500
about what that role entails

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.167
and maybe some things
about the organization itself.

00:09:41.167 --> 00:09:43.042
-I would love to.

00:09:43.042 --> 00:09:44.667
So my background,

00:09:44.667 --> 00:09:48.250
I sort of went through life
as a literary editor,

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:52.625
and then about 10 years ago,
I pivoted, which was wonderful,

00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:55.625
into this role
as an executive director

00:09:55.625 --> 00:09:57.542
of a literary nonprofit.

00:09:57.542 --> 00:10:01.333
So Aspen Words is the literary
program of the Aspen Institute.

00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:05.708
We have events
and a writing workshop.

00:10:05.708 --> 00:10:08.083
We have
an author-speaking series.

00:10:08.083 --> 00:10:10.083
We have writing.

00:10:10.083 --> 00:10:12.500
We have a writer
in residency program.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:14.792
And one exciting program we do,

00:10:14.792 --> 00:10:19.250
which I started about
5 or 6 years ago,

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.208
is the Aspen Words
Literary Prize,

00:10:21.208 --> 00:10:25.250
which is a $35,000 award
for a work of fiction

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.750
that shines a spotlight
on a social issue.

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:32.083
But it's really
just a fantastic organization

00:10:32.083 --> 00:10:37.000
whose mission
is to inspire readers,

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.375
encourage writers, and connect
people through stories.

00:10:40.375 --> 00:10:41.875
-That's fantastic.

00:10:41.875 --> 00:10:43.292
I hope people get a chance
to check that out,

00:10:43.292 --> 00:10:46.625
as well as your novel,
which is so great.

00:10:46.625 --> 00:10:48.542
Lastly, I wanted to ask,
is there a book you'd like

00:10:48.542 --> 00:10:52.000
to recommend for our listeners
to pick up this summer?

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.542
-I just finished
Claire Dederer's "Monsters,"

00:10:56.542 --> 00:10:58.000
and it is such a brilliant book.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.750
It is nonfiction
and it's essentially

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:04.792
this really thoughtful
reflection

00:11:04.792 --> 00:11:10.833
on what to do
with famous artists

00:11:10.833 --> 00:11:12.500
who are also
monstrous people.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:16.667
So like the Picassos
of the world,

00:11:16.667 --> 00:11:21.000
the Roman Polanskis,
but she is just so reflective

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.375
and so thoughtful
about how she approaches it.

00:11:23.375 --> 00:11:26.333
And it's something I think
we're all thinking about.

00:11:26.333 --> 00:11:27.833
-Absolutely.
-That sounds fascinating.

00:11:27.833 --> 00:11:29.333
I'll have to check
that out, too.

00:11:29.333 --> 00:11:31.167
-She's a great writer.

00:11:31.167 --> 00:11:32.375
-Well, thank you again,
Adrienne.

00:11:32.375 --> 00:11:33.792
It was so nice to talk to you

00:11:33.792 --> 00:11:35.667
and thank you for taking
the time to join us.

00:11:35.667 --> 00:11:37.333
-Thank you.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.792
-This has been "Late Night Lit."

00:11:41.792 --> 00:11:45.083
My thanks to Seth Meyers,
Mike Shoemaker,

00:11:45.083 --> 00:11:48.583
Adrienne Brodeur,
Jay Johnson, Ross Leupold,

00:11:48.583 --> 00:11:51.667
Maria Torres
and Raphael Aguilos.

00:11:51.667 --> 00:11:53.792
Our theme music was written
and performed

00:11:53.792 --> 00:11:55.125
by the 8G band.

00:11:55.125 --> 00:11:57.083
For "Late Night
with Seth Meyers,"

00:11:57.083 --> 00:11:58.500
I'm Sarah Jenks-Daly.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.083
Thanks for listening
and happy reading.

